389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 792 0 R
/T (Shared encoder design. The context branch reuses the target LLM's own frozen backbone no separate encoder copy, and its KV cache is shared with the LLM's prefill. Compared against a dedicated embedding model for context, both perform comparably and beat mean-transition and identity-transition baselines. \(addressing challenge #3\))
/E (Shared encoder design. The context branch reuses the target LLM's own frozen backbone no separate encoder copy, and its KV cache is shared with the LLM's prefill. Compared against a dedicated embedding model for context, both perform comparably and beat mean-transition and identity-transition baselines. \(addressing challenge #3\))
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
390 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 793 0 R
/T (Canonicalized chain-of-thought traces. Real CoT interleaves genuine steps with meta-thoughts \("Okay, let me try to figure this out", "Hmm, maybe I should check this again"\), self-corrections, and restatements. We rewrite each trace offline into clean, numbered logical steps before encoding so the predictor learns logic transitions rather than narration artifacts, and the position-derived halt labels stay clean. \(addressing challenge #4\))
/E (Canonicalized chain-of-thought traces. Real CoT interleaves genuine steps with meta-thoughts \("Okay, let me try to figure this out", "Hmm, maybe I should check this again"\), self-corrections, and restatements. We rewrite each trace offline into clean, numbered logical steps before encoding so the predictor learns logic transitions rather than narration artifacts, and the position-derived halt labels stay clean. \(addressing challenge #4\))
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
